  "source": {
    "display_title": "Mindfulness Training Modifies Subsystems of Attention",
    "authors": [
      "Amishi P. Jha",
      "Jason Krompinger",
      "Michael J. Baime"
    ]
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Mindfulness training can improve attention-related behavior by changing specific attentional subsystems rather than producing a single global attention benefit.",
      "short_title": "Training modifies attention subsystems",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The study examined alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring with the Attention Network Test before and after mindfulness training, and the authors report different post-training effects for MBSR and retreat participants."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "An 8-week mindfulness-based stress reduction course improves endogenous orienting relative to control and retreat groups at post-training testing.",
      "short_title": "MBSR improves orienting",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "An ANOVA for the RT difference scores of the orienting component revealed a significant factor of group [F(2,48) = 10.13, p < .001]. A series of contrasts revealed that the MBSR group differed from the control group and the retreat group."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A 1-month intensive mindfulness retreat among previously experienced meditators improves exogenous alerting-related detection when no target cue is provided.",
      "short_title": "Retreat improves alerting",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "At Time 2, the alerting difference score was significantly smaller for the retreat group than for the combined control and MBSR groups, and no-cue response times were significantly shorter in the younger retreat subgroup."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Participants with prior concentrative meditation experience show reduced conflict-monitoring interference at baseline relative to meditation-naive participants.",
      "short_title": "Experience reduces conflict",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "At Time 1, the retreat group differed from the combined control and MBSR participants for conflict monitoring in RT [t(1,49) = 2.22, p = .03] and accuracy [t(1,49) = 5.62, p < .001]."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Post-training conflict-monitoring differences are not detected among the MBSR, retreat, and control groups, possibly because task repetition improves conflict scores in controls.",
      "short_title": "Conflict gains fade",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "An ANOVA for the conflict monitoring component RT and accuracy difference scores revealed no significant main effects and no significant effects on any of the paired contrasts (p > .36)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The Attention Network Test separates alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring by subtracting performance across predefined cue and target conditions.",
      "short_title": "ANT partitions attention",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Alerting was indexed by subtracting performance measures on double-cue trials from those on no-cue trials; orienting by subtracting spatial-cue trials from center-cue trials; and conflict monitoring by subtracting congruent target trials from incongruent target trials."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Within the retreat group, greater prior meditation experience predicts a smaller Time 2 alerting score, consistent with a more readied attentional state.",
      "short_title": "Experience predicts alerting",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The correlation value (R = -.52) was significant (t = 2.4, p = .03), indicating a significant reduction in alerting score with increased meditation experience in the retreat group."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The attentional effects should be treated as preliminary because the study used small groups, no active-training control, behavioral measures only, and imperfect control over training protocols.",
      "short_title": "Findings require caution",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The discussion states that the results should be interpreted cautiously, that the training groups included relatively small numbers of participants, and that future work should include an active-training control group and neural measurements."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_items": [
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-ant-subtraction-indices--0f4e7a9c2b11",
      "title": "ANT Difference Scores",
      "excerpt": "All analyses were performed on RT scores for correct trials only and accuracy scores. Alerting was indexed by subtracting performance measures on double-cue trials from those on no-cue trials. Orienting was indexed by subtracting performance measures on spatial-cue trials from those on center-cue trials. Conflict monitoring was indexed by subtracting performance measures on congruent target trials from those on incongruent target trials.",
      "overview": "This methods passage defines the measurement structure used for all main claims. It shows that the study did not treat attention as a single undifferentiated outcome, but mapped behavioral response-time and accuracy differences onto alerting, orienting, and conflict-monitoring subsystems. The definitions also limit interpretation to ANT-derived behavioral indices rather than direct neural measurements.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "0",
        "5"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.112"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-training-group-doses--8b2d4a6f90c3",
      "title": "Training Group Doses",
      "excerpt": "The MBSR group included 17 meditation-naive participants in an 8-week course that met once weekly for 3 h and assigned 30 min of daily sitting meditation. The retreat group participated in a full-time 1-month residential mindfulness retreat, had prior concentrative meditation experience ranging from 4 to 360 months, and engaged in formal mindfulness meditation practices for 10-12 h per day.",
      "overview": "These participant and training descriptions ground the comparison between novice MBSR participants, experienced retreat participants, and meditation-naive controls. They matter because the authors interpret different ANT changes as consequences of different training histories and practice intensities. The evidence also marks a limitation: groups differed not only in intervention type but also in prior experience and training dose.",
      "evidence_type": "measurement",
      "claim_refs": [
        "0",
        "1",
        "2",
        "6",
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.111"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-baseline-conflict-reduction--c7a91f02de45",
      "title": "Baseline Conflict Reduction",
      "excerpt": "At Time 1, the first contrast between the MBSR group and the control group revealed no significant conflict-monitoring difference. The second contrast, comparing the collapsed meditation-naive groups with the retreat group, revealed a significant difference for RT [t(1,49) = 2.22, p = .03] and accuracy [t(1,49) = 5.62, p < .001], with reduced flanker interference for retreat participants.",
      "overview": "This statistical result supports the claim that prior meditation experience was associated with better baseline conflict monitoring. Because the retreat group was already experienced before the formal study intervention, the result is evidence about prior concentrative meditation experience rather than a randomized post-training effect. It helps separate baseline expertise from changes observed after MBSR or retreat participation.",
      "evidence_type": "statistical_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "3"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.113",
        "Figure 2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-mbsr-orienting-anova--3b6f8d0a4c12",
      "title": "MBSR Orienting ANOVA",
      "excerpt": "At Time 2, an ANOVA for the RT difference scores of the orienting component revealed a significant factor of group [F(2,48) = 10.13, p < .001]. Planned contrasts showed that the MBSR group differed from the control group (Bonferroni-adjusted p = .046) and the retreat group (Bonferroni-adjusted p < .001), while the retreat and control groups did not differ.",
      "overview": "This is the central statistical evidence for the MBSR orienting claim. The finding indicates that the novice mindfulness course changed the ANT orienting score relative to both no-training controls and the intensive retreat group. The result is restricted to response-time difference scores; the same passage reports no significant group effect for accuracy difference scores.",
      "evidence_type": "statistical_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "1"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.114",
        "Figure 3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-retreat-alerting-contrast--92da34bc781e",
      "title": "Retreat Alerting Contrast",
      "excerpt": "At Time 2, planned contrasts for alerting found no significant MBSR-control difference, but the collapsed control and MBSR groups differed from the retreat group in RT scores [t(1,49) = 3.55, p < .001]. No-cue RTs were significantly shorter in the younger retreat group than in the control plus MBSR group [t(1,40) = 2.17, p = .03], whereas double-cue RTs were not significantly different.",
      "overview": "This result supports the interpretation that retreat participation altered alerting-related performance by improving target detection when no temporal or spatial cue was available. The double-cue non-difference narrows the effect to no-cue readiness rather than a general speed advantage across alerting conditions. The evidence is strongest for the younger retreat subgroup when explicit RT comparisons are made because the full retreat group had broader age variation.",
      "evidence_type": "statistical_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "2"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.115",
        "Figure 4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-experience-alerting-correlation--e19a03df4b67",
      "title": "Experience Alerting Correlation",
      "excerpt": "Since only the alerting component significantly changed at Time 2 for the retreat group, the authors examined whether months of meditation experience correlated with the magnitude of the alerting score at Time 2. The correlation value was R = -.52 and significant (t = 2.4, p = .03), showing reduced alerting score with increased meditation experience.",
      "overview": "The correlation links individual differences in prior meditation experience to the retreat group's alerting outcome. It strengthens the interpretation that the Time 2 alerting pattern is related to meditation history, not merely group membership. Its scope is limited because the analysis is correlational within the retreat group and does not establish a randomized causal dose-response effect.",
      "evidence_type": "statistical_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "6"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.115"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-task-repetition-and-limits--a8d201ceb934",
      "title": "Task Repetition Limits",
      "excerpt": "An ANOVA on overall RT and accuracy collapsed across conditions showed a significant main effect of time [F(1,16) = 13.6, p < .001], with RTs faster at Time 2 than at Time 1, but no significant group interaction. The discussion adds that conflict monitoring differences were absent at Time 2 and that the study should be interpreted cautiously because training groups were small and future studies need active controls and neural measurements.",
      "overview": "This evidence captures the main constraint on the paper's interpretation. Practice or task-exposure effects can improve performance over repeated ANT sessions, especially for conflict monitoring, which may obscure group-specific training effects. The limitations also caution against treating the behavioral findings as a complete mechanism without active-control, neural, and more tightly standardized intervention designs.",
      "evidence_type": "statistical_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "4",
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.115",
        "pp.116-117"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "relations": [],
  "summary": "Jha, Krompinger, and Baime report that mindfulness training changes ANT-measured attention subsystems in different ways depending on training history. Novice MBSR participants showed improved orienting after an 8-week course, experienced retreat participants showed altered alerting and faster no-cue detection after a 1-month retreat, and experienced meditators already showed reduced conflict interference at baseline. The findings are behavioral and preliminary, with important limits from group size, prior-experience differences, task repetition, and lack of active-training controls.",
